#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbd9cf26-36a3-573f-be14-4292f29ef821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347755} {:answer/id #uuid "19c9af6e-fd42-5fe8-a8a0-4ef5c76c9b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347756} {:answer/id #uuid "d79405e0-cf4f-56f0-ba10-aaf9cddf88c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347757} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3f6f05-e2b9-5329-9364-d0192d174f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347758} {:answer/id #uuid "0d66860a-50b2-5812-a611-399d148a0e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c9af6e-fd42-5fe8-a8a0-4ef5c76c9b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347756} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfba4061-161c-5e71-9c8e-3ddfb8354902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28b07468-67e6-5160-b480-58f04dacf3f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126835" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨3t+5,1-{t}^{3}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)+5,1-{0}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨3,-3(0)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aac0b62f-82a5-5f6b-87fb-418c28c4f0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116770} {:answer/id #uuid "d63846d8-45af-588b-b6fe-a9c61df6d1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116771} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1a4263-862f-5307-aa97-94f2060f3dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116772} {:answer/id #uuid "6194ce5e-78b1-5b50-a21a-1db63f823829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116773} {:answer/id #uuid "78e821a1-993c-55b8-ba51-bc0545154590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aac0b62f-82a5-5f6b-87fb-418c28c4f0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116770} :answer-field/id #uuid "02306244-ff8d-59f9-b3b4-753c983e75ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186116769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22d6e6f4-daf0-5645-a375-f0c335545e70" :question/math-academy-id "q-126902" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}-t,5-3{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t-1,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,2⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,2⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =⟨-c,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4t-1=-c \\\\ -6t=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=-4t+1.$ Substituting $c=-4t+1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6t & =2(-4t+1) \\\\ -6t & =-8t+2 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{2}-1,5-3(1)^{2}⟩=⟨1,2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "297a4812-a87a-592a-818a-8c8d3d3f7868" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0ba96f-fca2-5b85-a4a8-c7ca577b4743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,4)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347762} {:answer/id #uuid "e2033a63-1ab3-5f1e-99af-a83fcad0e314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(7,9)$ and $(14,16)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347763} {:answer/id #uuid "27768cb2-e09c-5f1e-863e-f00fbca3eafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347764} {:answer/id #uuid "2de6936d-188d-5b1b-92bb-57ef40e0405f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347765} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe7a385-9e89-5968-927b-cfb29a8fb0d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(14,16)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2de6936d-188d-5b1b-92bb-57ef40e0405f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347765} :answer-field/id #uuid "43088988-e045-502f-b9da-394309ab53c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d4944e3-e48d-5ad3-888c-09e6883cda6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164840" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j]\\cdot (2t\\,i+2t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2)\\cdot 2t+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 2t(2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 4t({t}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 4t(t-1)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =((1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(1)^{2}\\,j=-\\,i+j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73b04b7-7f81-5da6-b3eb-9ceb86cb6994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1, 2)$ and $(8, e, 2e)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117403} {:answer/id #uuid "cc024178-9448-5f3b-8037-94e2b89c36c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1, 1, 2)$ and $(8, e^{2}, 2e^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117404} {:answer/id #uuid "05378c03-7088-58e5-8833-839871a0f537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1, 1)$ and $(4, e, 2e)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117405} {:answer/id #uuid "34c8f45b-2edd-5e5b-9567-319bcce99dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1, 2)$ and $(8, e^{2}, 2e^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117406} {:answer/id #uuid "47f31d44-dbcf-5cb3-8b68-b2659ef75f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1, 1)$ and $(8, e^{2}, 2e^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34c8f45b-2edd-5e5b-9567-319bcce99dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 1, 2)$ and $(8, e^{2}, 2e^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117406} :answer-field/id #uuid "18987b7e-5379-5dd3-a051-c78ae8fbfe35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e918f3b-d930-5ea5-9b21-8651bb576465" :question/math-academy-id "q-32918" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =c\\,⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =⟨4ct,\\,c{e}^{t},\\,2c{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=4ct \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}=2c{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} & =1\\cdot 4t \\\\ {t}^{2} & =2t \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2},\\,{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{2},\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e28b26-0eb7-51ed-bcf9-2660b98a6028" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Line to a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c431266-4c36-5efa-9d12-b3c03dd77f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,-1,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347769} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb029d8-f44a-57f8-9b2a-b0c8da08d92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347770} {:answer/id #uuid "11a92c0f-7f2f-5703-bcca-37d080f7ea57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347771} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2dc8ff-4b91-51e3-af02-cc5df3908b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347772} {:answer/id #uuid "8919beb2-2605-58ed-839d-0c399b920152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8919beb2-2605-58ed-839d-0c399b920152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347773} :answer-field/id #uuid "288cbfdd-3be2-5693-8506-6a82d50d6f5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f79a0634-b781-513c-bc7a-42650fc401f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75203" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}]}
